    {
      "id": "claim_b397c13345a241ee9a2fdaffd2a5f2b6",
      "statement": "Owner notification for queued friend requests goes through the Notifier interface, which must never raise: ConsoleNotifier (the server default) prints the actionable card to stderr; WebhookNotifier POSTs the card as HMAC-SHA256-signed JSON with an X-HAAP-Signature: sha256=<hex> header and a 5-second timeout, designed to target a Hermes webhook that lands the approval card in the owner's chat; CompositeNotifier fans out to several. Notification failures are swallowed so they never break the protocol.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://haap/policy.py#L141-L205",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:1412eb0658e5b746d3762f21e3813d8a59c4c7bd1d5cecb943f4d2a81f7b0bcc:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ea257feb4e16473aba2f07b8efcc022c",
      "statement": "Although HAAPServer stores an on_friend_request callback and the README and ARQUITECTURA describe that callback pushing requests to the owner's Hermes chat (Matrix/Telegram), the implemented friend_request queue path notifies only through the configured notifier (self.notifier.notify(request_card)); no code path currently invokes on_friend_request, so that native Hermes approval wiring remains documented design rather than a running integration.",
