    {
      "id": "claim_0d1d709057fd4a62abd9c3f6ea5d185d",
      "statement": "HAAP aligns with the A2A standard on discovery (public manifest at /.well-known/haap.json mirrors the A2A Agent Card pattern at /.well-known/agent-card.json) and lifecycle (identical task state names), while differing on transport (own signed envelope versus JSON-RPC 2.0 + SSE) and security (challenge-response friendship with human approval and granular permissions versus A2A OpenAPI/API-key schemes) — the trust layer being HAAP's stated differentiator, since A2A ships no trust layer of its own. A future A2A-HAAP bridge (exposing the manifest as an Agent Card and translating JSON-RPC to envelopes) is documented and deliberately not implemented in v1.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/ARQUITECTURA.md#L273-L286",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:79890723d26f2107564cbb1424a855d4fc165f7cced1be989bc207a9c8f74385:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_ffe1e64000814b4496842e294b19df4d",
      "statement": "Task lifecycle states use A2A names (submitted, accepted, working, completed, failed, rejected) and are enforced locally per agent by an explicit transition table: submitted goes to accepted/rejected/failed; accepted to working/completed/failed/rejected; working to working/completed/failed/rejected; completed/failed/rejected are terminal. Invalid transitions raise TaskStateError, and the registry is local per agent, storing both tasks it delegated and tasks it executed.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://haap/tasks.py#L22-L25",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a7b257421a8aa315f9b1ade47377d2f13470fd39088a0dae9a6dfdb21f85b620:"
